  "page1": {
    "title": "The Typos That Shaped Every Living Thing",
    "subtitle": "Why a single changed letter can matter so much.",
    "body": "Your body runs on a set of instructions written in a four-letter chemical alphabet. These instructions are copied faithfully every time a cell divides. But no copying machine is perfect, and once in a while a single letter is changed, dropped, or added. We call such a change a <u>mutation</u>, and despite its frightening reputation, it is simply a typo in the genetic text. Most of these typos are harmless, landing in a spot where the meaning barely shifts.\n\nThe real effect of a mutation depends almost entirely on where it falls and what it happens to touch. A change in a long, unused stretch of DNA may quietly do nothing at all. But a change in one crucial instruction can alter an important protein. That can reshape a visible trait, and sometimes even cause serious disease. Yet the very same process is also the quiet engine of variety. Once in a great while, a random change happens to be useful, giving its owner a tiny advantage in survival. Over countless generations, such rare lucky <mark>errors</mark> build up, and this is the raw material on which evolution slowly works. A small change in fur color, for instance, might hide an animal better from a hunting predator. Across thousands of years, that tiny edge can reshape an entire species.\n\nThis double nature is what makes mutation so striking. The very same blind, mindless process that can quietly break a healthy body is also the only true source of genuinely new traits. It is the deep reason life is not frozen but endlessly inventive. Without faithful copying, life simply could not continue at all. Yet without occasional copying mistakes, it could never <blank>. Perfection and progress, it quietly turns out, pull in opposite directions.",
    "illustration": "../../assets/illustrations/2026-07-J/10.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "A mutation is a typo in the genetic text — sometimes harmful, sometimes the seed of something new.",
    "gloss_extra": [
      { "term": "protein", "ko": "단백질" },
      { "term": "trait", "ko": "형질, 특성" },
      { "term": "evolution", "ko": "진화" }
    ]
  },
